        "math_academy_id": "q-107509",
        "knowledge_point_id": "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36",
        "knowledge_point": "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form",
        "problem": "Express $z=\\frac{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})}{(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+2\\text{i}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i})}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\text{i}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\text{i}}{2},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$",
