        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points",
        "problem": "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(-4,3,1)$, $Q(-1,2,0)$, and $R(-10,5,3).$",
        "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨-4,3,1⟩,\\,q=⟨-1,2,0⟩,\\,r=⟨-10,5,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨-4,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨-10,5,3⟩-⟨-4,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot (-6)+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22}{\\sqrt{11}\\cdot \\sqrt{44}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}.\n$$",
